  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Nanluoguxiang is the most accessible introduction to Beijing's hutong culture — the network of narrow alleyways and courtyard homes (四合院, siheyuan) that formed the residential fabric of the imperial capital for 700 years. The main lane runs 786 metres north to south, lined with converted courtyard homes turned into cafes, boutiques, and snack stalls, with 8 historic side alleys branching east and west into quieter residential territory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hutongs are the opposite of the Forbidden City in every sense: small-scale, informal, domestic, accidental. The word comes from Mongolian (hottog, meaning "water well") and the layout dates to the Yuan Dynasty (1271–1368), when Beijing was planned as a grid of alleyways radiating from the imperial palace. The hutong system at its peak covered most of inner Beijing. Less than a quarter survives — the rest was demolished in successive waves of urban development through the 20th century.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nanluoguxiang itself became commercially developed in the 2000s and is now unambiguously touristy on the main lane. But the side hutongs — Mao'er Hutong, Ju'er Hutong, Qiangu Hutong — remain genuinely residential, and walking into them reveals the actual texture of hutong life: shared courtyards, improvised extensions, vegetable gardens in gaps between walls, elderly residents playing chess outside their doors. The contrast between the main lane and the side alleys is part of what makes the area worth visiting.
  </p>

  <h2>What to See & Do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Walk the main lane</strong> — north to south (or reverse), stopping for snacks: fried stinky tofu, yoghurt in clay pots, tanghulu (sugar-coated hawthorn berries on skewers), cold noodles. The commercial strip is lively and photogenic even if it's no longer authentic.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn into the side hutongs</strong> — Mao'er Hutong (帽儿胡同) to the east is one of the most intact, with several well-preserved courtyard mansions including the former residence of Wanrong, the last Empress of China.</li>
    <li><strong>Rickshaw tour</strong> — touts operate from the main lane entrance. Negotiate the price before getting in. A 45-minute ride through multiple hutongs gives more ground coverage than walking and the driver usually provides (basic) commentary.</li>
    <li><strong>Yandai Xiejie (烟袋斜街)</strong> — a diagonal hutong near the north end, one of the oldest commercial streets in Beijing, now specialising in antiques, pipes, and small curiosities.</li>
    <li><strong>Houhai Lake (后海)</strong> — 5min walk from the north end of the main lane. A willow-lined lake with bars and restaurants on its bank, popular in the evening. Good for a drink before heading to the train station.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best Time to Visit</h2>
  <p>
    Late afternoon (3–6pm) — the light is golden, the temperature drops slightly, and the evening crowd gives the lane a good energy without being oppressive. Avoid midday in July — the lane is narrow, sheltered, and becomes a heat trap. Weekdays are quieter than weekends.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Entry:</strong> Free. No tickets.</li>
    <li><strong>Getting there:</strong> 5min walk from Nanluoguxiang subway station (Line 6 / Line 8). By car, park at the edges of the hutong area — no driving inside the alleys.</li>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> 1.5–2 hours for the main lane and a few side alleys. Add 30min if going to Houhai.</li>
    <li><strong>Food:</strong> Eat at the stalls on the main lane — the snack culture is genuine even if the shops around them are not. Avoid the sit-down restaurants on the main lane (overpriced). The side hutongs have better local spots.</li>
    <li><strong>Shopping:</strong> Small independent boutiques selling Chinese-design products, vintage items, calligraphy supplies. Quality varies. Bargaining is not standard here (fixed prices), but you can ask.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why It's Worth It</h2>
  <p>
    The hutongs are what Beijing looked like before the 20th century arrived. Walking through them — even the commercialised version — gives first-time visitors to China a scale and texture that the Forbidden City, for all its grandeur, cannot provide: ordinary life, small doors, grey brick, the smell of food cooking, children running between walls. It is the human dimension of a city that can otherwise feel overwhelmingly monumental.
  </p>
